<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alutiiq</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sugpiaq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alutiiq, or Sugpiaq, are an Indigenous people of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, living on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak Island</a>, the Alaska Peninsula and the coasts of Prince William Sound. In 1784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-shelikhov">Grigory Shelikhov</a>'s men massacred many of them at Refuge Rock, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a> they were forced to hunt sea otters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alutiiq.jpg" alt="Alutiiq" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christopher Mertl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlutiiqDancer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Alutiiq are a Yupik people, one of eight groups of Alaska Natives, and are also called Pacific Eskimo or Pacific Yupik. Their traditional homelands go back more than 7,500 years and include Prince William Sound and the outer Kenai Peninsula, home of the Chugach Sugpiaq, and the Kodiak Archipelago and Alaska Peninsula, home of the Koniag Alutiiq. In the early 1800s the Kodiak archipelago alone held more than 60 Alutiiq villages with an estimated 13,000 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Alutiiq comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleut">Aleut</a>, the word the promyshlenniki, the Siberian and Russian fur traders and settlers, applied to the peoples of the region. Russian occupation began in 1784 after the Awa'uq Massacre, in which the fur trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-shelikhov">Grigory Shelikhov</a> killed hundreds of Sugpiat at Refuge Rock off Sitkalidak Island, near the present village of Old Harbor. Because of that violent colonial past, some Alaska Natives of the region prefer their own word, Sugpiaq, meaning the real people; Alutiiq, Aleut and Sugpiaq are all in use.</p><p>More than 4,000 Alutiiq live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> today, in coastal fishing communities, and federally recognised tribes include the Alutiiq Tribe of Old Harbor, the Native Village of Karluk and the Sun'aq Tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak</a>. Their language belongs to the Yup'ik branch of the Eskaleut languages. Its Kodiak dialect had only about 50 speakers, all elderly, when the high school in Kodiak agreed in 2010 to teach it at the request of Alutiiq students.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditional life follows the coast and the seasons. Spring, after the lean winter, is the hardest time, when people gather shellfish, hunt octopus and pick greens at low tide; then they fish for cod and halibut, collect herring eggs and hunt seals. Summer brings work on the open ocean and at sea lion haulouts and bird rookeries, and trade with the mainland for antler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and caribou pelts. In autumn they harvest salmon and berries and hunt bears before preserving food for the winter storms, spent mostly indoors with festivals for the harvest and the ancestors.</p><p>Before contact with Russian fur traders they lived in semi-subterranean houses called ciqlluaq. Their arts include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> of shell, bone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and amber, woodcarving tied to Alutiiq religion, sewn skin clothing and grass weaving, while storytelling, song and dance pass on history in the absence of a traditional system of writing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alutiiq” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name Lingít means 'People of the Tides', while the old Russian name for them, Koloshi, came from a Sugpiaq-<b>Alutiiq</b> word for the labret worn by women.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tlingit">Tlingit · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Aleut and <b>Alutiiq</b> hunters were forced to hunt sea otters, whose pelts were sold to China.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
